    "translation": "Cyrus travels from Sardis, and the army marches through Lydia. Those traveling carry their weapons. Xenophon examines the route with Proxenus.\n\nThe army arrives at the Maeander. Over the river is a bridge made of boats. The soldiers walk over the bridge; those preparing keep watch over weapons and resources.\n\nIn Phrygia the army travels to Colossae and then arrives at Celaenae. There Cyrus has buildings and a park. The Maeander flows through the park.\n\nOther contingents come to Cyrus. Clearchus leads hoplites, peltasts, and archers. The soldiers who are preparing are in the park, and Cyrus reviews the army.\n\nWhile traveling, Xenophon sees his friends. The friends discuss the route: “The army uses extensive preparation,” they say. Others prepare their own belongings.\n\nThe army arrives at Peltae. Xenias prepares a sacrifice and games in honor of Lycaean Zeus. The athletes compete, and Cyrus watches the games. Golden strigils are the prizes.\n\nThose competing make their friends rejoice, but the soldiers also ask for pay. They arrive at Caystru-pedion. There Cyrus owes more than three months’ pay.\n\nThe soldiers repeatedly come to Cyrus and ask for their pay. Cyrus speaks of hopes, but the money is not yet available. Xenophon sees the soldiers’ effort; those marching need food and pay.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 1.2.5–11. https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Aabo%3Atlg%2C0032%2C006%3A1%3A2",
