    "translation": "Now the account concerns Athens, not Cyrus’s journey. After the battle at Munychia the citizens carry the dead. Cleocritus, the herald of the initiates, approaches the other citizens.\n\nCleocritus comes to announce his judgment. He has a fine voice, and the citizens are silent. “Fellow citizens,” he says, “why do you drive us from the city? Why do you want to kill us?”\n\n“We share rites, sacrifices, and festivals. At the festivals we dance together, and we share the same education. In war too we are comrades; we guard our common freedom.”\n\n“Kinship binds many of us, and friendship is strong. Some of the dead are ours, others yours. We weep also for your dead. We regard you as citizens, not enemies.”\n\n“It is necessary to save our homeland; we must not obey the Thirty. For the Thirty seek their private advantage. We shall seek our common safety and judge what is just.”\n\nCleocritus delivers his speech, but the officials lead their own men away. Not everyone immediately becomes a friend. Some listen, others depart; the city is still in danger.\n\nAgain we consider leaders. For what purpose do the citizens go? For what purpose will the soldiers fight? “I shall go to ask about safety” is a statement of the person inquiring, not a statement of Cleocritus. Not every leader has the same purpose.\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Hellenica 2.4.19–22. Controlled course adaptation; dialogue and teaching futures are newly supplied, not verbatim quotations. https://www.magalia.wiki/historiae/fulltext/xenophon-hellenica.html https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1174/1174-h/1174-h.htm",
    "notesMarkdown": "Course adaptation of Xenophon, Hellenica 2.4.19–22. Shared worship, education, military service, family ties, grief, and the appeal against obedience to the Thirty follow the source. The Greek wording, statements of future intention, and purpose participles are newly supplied teaching adaptations. They are not preserved quotations from Cleocritus."
