  "wordStudy": {
    "label": "Word Study — Calling, considering, accepting",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "title": "Calling, considering, accepting",
        "practiceTopic": "word-study",
        "body": [
          "καλέω brings officers together; σκέπτομαι and λογίζομαι describe consideration and reasoning. Their aorists ἐκάλεσεν, ἐσκέψατο, and ἐλογίσατο mark actions in the narrative, rather than portraying initiative as an unexplained burst of confidence.",
          "δέχομαι can mean receive or accept. Here ἐδέξαντο τὴν βουλήν means the captains accepted a proposal. Its middle form is translated with an active English verb; the captains are not being accepted by someone else.",
          "κοινός means shared, and μόνος means alone. The clause οὐ μόνος ὁ Ξενοφῶν ἔπραξεν prevents an exaggerated claim of sole leadership. The separate Socratic comparison asks whether understanding danger is necessary for ἀνδρεία."
        ],
        "display": [
          {
            "greek": "ἐκάλεσεν",
            "english": "he summoned"
          },
          {
            "greek": "ἐσκέψατο",
            "english": "he considered"
          },
          {
            "greek": "ἐλογίσατο",
            "english": "he reasoned"
          },
          {
            "greek": "ἐδέξαντο",
            "english": "they accepted"
          },
          {
            "greek": "κοινός / μόνος",
            "english": "shared / alone"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
