    "translation": "The Greeks crossed the river and began the march. They placed the baggage animals in the middle. Their enemies sent horsemen and archers.\n\nThe enemies kept shooting from a distance. The Greeks pursued them, but did not catch them. Xenophon led the soldiers back toward the army’s main body.\n\nThe other generals criticized Xenophon. On hearing, he accepted the criticism. The pursuit had not saved the soldiers; a different preparation was necessary.\n\nXenophon spoke about the Rhodians. These men have slings and use lead. The generals identified those able to sling and allocated money to them.\n\nTwo hundred slingers prepared themselves. The generals also gathered horses. On the next day they organized fifty horsemen; Lycius commanded the cavalry.\n\nThe Greeks made their preparations and remained. They had not yet reached the sea or arrived home. Now they prepare the route through shared deliberation.\n\nIn Memorabilia Socrates speaks about a general. A leader must care for the others. He wants not only to fare well himself, but also for those following him to fare well.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 3.3.1–20; separate companion, Memorabilia 3.2.1–4. https://cais-soas.com/CAIS/History/hakhamaneshian/xenophon/anabasis/xeno_anbs_book_3.htm https://monadnock.net/xenophon/mem3-2.html",
    "notesMarkdown": "The simplified Greek is newly composed for this course. It preserves the source distinctions explained above while reducing sentence complexity. The English translation aligns paragraph for paragraph. Supplementary grammar examples and commands illustrate forms and are not additional historical claims."
