        "Asigmatic first aorists: liquids and nasals: Verb stems ending in λ, μ, ν, or ρ often have first-aorist endings without a visible σ. Historically σ is lost and the preceding vowel is lengthened or otherwise altered. μένω gives ἔμεινα; νέμω gives ἔνειμα. These are first aorists, not second aorists merely because σ is absent.",
        "Irregular first-aorist stems and principal parts: The sigmatic pattern does not make every stem predictable from its present form. καίω, “burn,” forms ἔκαυσα; κλαίω, “weep,” forms ἔκλαυσα. Their αυ before σ must be learned with the principal parts, rather than generated by retaining the present -αι-.",
        "Augment in compound verbs: In a compound verb the augment normally comes after the preverb and before the verb stem. Thus συν- + ἔλεξα becomes συνέλεξα, while παρα- + ἐσκευασάμην gives παρεσκευασάμην. The preverb keeps its directional or relational contribution to the meaning.",
        "Cumulative review: nouns, adjectives, and reference: Module II expanded noun systems: third-declension velar, dental, labial, nasal, liquid, and -ντ- stems; neuters in -μα; vowel stems πόλις and ἄστυ; diphthong patterns βασιλεύς, ναῦς, and βοῦς; family nouns πατήρ and μήτηρ; irregular γυνή and χείρ. A genitive reveals the stem, while gender and agreement identify the words connected in the sentence. σώφρων, σῶφρον has a common masculine/feminine form and a separate neuter; πᾶς, πᾶσα, πᾶν agrees in all three genders. The article can turn an adjective into a substantive: οἱ ἀνδρεῖοι means “the brave.”",
        "Cumulative review: participles, futures, and narrative: Present active participles such as μένων and present middle πορευόμενος describe an action as ongoing. They agree with their head noun. With the article, ὁ λέγων means “the speaker / person speaking”; ὁ λέγων στρατηγός is “the general who is speaking.” A simple circumstantial λέγων can mean “while speaking.” Present participles express ongoing action relative to the main verb, not an independent calendar date. First-aorist λύσας / λυσάμενος and second-aorist λαβών / λαβόμενος view their actions as wholes, often before the main action in a narrative."
