    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Building the imperfect active: The imperfect indicative presents a past action from within: it was happening, used to happen, or kept happening. Add the augment to the present stem, then secondary endings. A consonant-initial stem takes ἐ-; an initial vowel normally lengthens.",
        "2. Imperfect middle and deponent verbs: Middle and passive imperfects share one set of endings: -όμην, -ου, -ετο, -όμεθα, -εσθε, -οντο. Their meaning depends on the verb and context. A deponent such as διαλέγομαι retains its ordinary sense, “converse.”",
        "3. Contract verbs and εἰμί in the past: Contract verbs retain their contraction rules after augmentation: ἐποίεε becomes ἐποίει, and ἐποίεον becomes ἐποίουν. The latter can mean “I was making” or “they were making.” Identify person from context.",
        "4. Choosing a viewpoint: imperfect and aorist: Imperfect and aorist are both past in the indicative, but they offer different views. ἤσθιεν shows eating in progress; ἦλθεν presents an arrival as a whole. Neither tense tells us by itself how many minutes an event lasted."
      ]
    }
