        "2. Asking: τίς and τί: Accented τίς asks “who?” or “which?”; neuter τί asks “what?” A question word can be a pronoun on its own or an adjective with a noun. The case shows its function: τίνα βίον αἱρεῖ; “Which life do you choose?”",
        "3. Someone, something, a certain: Indefinite τις/τι means “someone/something” or “a certain/some” with a noun. It is enclitic: its accent normally depends on neighboring words. Unlike interrogative τίς, it does not itself ask a question.",
        "4. Where, how, and why?: Question adverbs do not decline: ποῦ asks “where?”, πόθεν “from where?”, ποῖ “to where?”, πῶς “how?”, and πότε “when?” Their indefinite partners include που, ποθεν, ποι, πως, ποτε."
