    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Present passive: who receives the action?: A passive verb makes the affected person or thing the grammatical subject. Compare ὁ φύλαξ φυλάττει τὴν γυναῖκα, “the guard protects the woman,” with ἡ γυνὴ φυλάττεται, “the woman is protected.” Present passive forms use the same endings as the present middle.",
        "2. Imperfect passive: action in progress: The imperfect passive uses augment, present stem, and middle/passive secondary endings. ἐφυλάττετο means “she was being protected” in the appropriate context. The imperfect can describe an ongoing state of custody without implying a completed rescue.",
        "3. Agent, instrument, and responsibility: A personal agent is commonly expressed by ὑπό plus genitive: ὑπὸ τοῦ φύλακος, “by the guard.” An instrument can be a bare dative: λίθῳ, “with a stone.” Keep these roles distinct from the person affected.",
        "4. Active, middle, passive, and deponent: Active, middle, and passive describe different relationships between a subject and an action. A middle may involve the subject’s own interest, as in choosing for oneself; a passive presents the subject as affected. Deponent verbs use middle forms with meanings such as “answer” or “wish.”"
      ]
    }
