    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. δίδωμι in the present and imperfect: δίδωμι is athematic: its endings attach to the stem without the ordinary thematic ο/ε. The present singular has a long vowel, δίδωμι, δίδως, δίδωσι; the plural has short ο, δίδομεν, δίδοτε, διδόασι.",
        "2. τίθημι in the present and imperfect: τίθημι means “put, place” and follows a long/short vowel pattern: τίθημι, τίθης, τίθησι; τίθεμεν, τίθετε, τιθέασι. It can also mean “set out” an example or proposal.",
        "3. Aorists: ἔδωκα and ἔθηκα: δίδωμι and τίθημι have aorist active singulars in -κα: ἔδωκα, ἔδωκας, ἔδωκε; ἔθηκα, ἔθηκας, ἔθηκε. Their ordinary Attic plurals use shorter stems: ἔδομεν, ἔδοτε, ἔδοσαν; ἔθεμεν, ἔθετε, ἔθεσαν.",
        "4. Commands, infinitives, and future forms: Learn a few high-frequency non-indicative forms as a usable set. Present διδόναι and τιθέναι view giving and placing as processes; aorist δοῦναι and θεῖναι view the actions as wholes. Aorist singular commands are δός and θές."
      ]
    }
