          "A genitive absolute supplies a circumstance with its own subject and participle, both in the genitive. τοῦ Σωκράτους λέγοντος means “while Socrates was speaking.” The phrase stands grammatically apart from the main clause: οἱ φίλοι ἤκουον, “the friends listened.”",
          "The participle agrees with its own genitive subject, not the main subject. “Absolute” means detached in construction, not unconnected in meaning. Translate naturally as while, when, since, or although as context supports; do not choose cause automatically."
