        "3. ἵστημι: make stand or stand?: Present active ἵστημι is normally transitive, “make stand, set up.” Its first aorist ἔστησα also causes someone or something to stand. The second aorist ἔστην is intransitive, “I stood.” This difference is lexical and grammatical, not merely a change of time.",
        "4. καθίστημι and ἀφίστημι: Compounds retain the important causative/intransitive contrast. καθίστημι can mean “appoint” someone to a role; κατέστησα means “I appointed,” while κατέστην can mean “I became/was established.” An appointment often uses two accusatives: the person and the role."
